{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>בְּסוֹדָם</b> etc. One needs to know the intent of the verse. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 106b): ״בְּסוֹדָם״ — this is the deed of Zimri; ״בִּקְהָלָם״ — this is the deed of Korah; ״כִּי בְאַפָּם״ etc. — this is Hamor and the men of Shechem; ״וּבִרְצוֹנָם״ etc. — this is Joseph, etc. (Targum Yerushalmi), end quote. And their words of blessed memory are in the manner of derash, but according to peshat it is difficult: why does he put the later event first and delay the deed of Shechem and Joseph, which came first? Further, how can the word <b>כִּי</b> be justified for a matter in its own right that has no connection to what precedes it? Further, why does he attribute the sin of Zimri also to Levi, when he is on the contrary the one who exacts vengeance? Further, one needs to know the intent of his saying <b>וּבִרְצוֹנָם</b> — why did he mention ratzon in this?" "<b>אָכֵן</b> the peshat of the verse is that the entire passage speaks of the deed of Joseph. And he said <b>בְּסוֹדָם</b> — meaning, from the time they began to hate him, and the matter was among them in secret counsel: when they devise a secret, ״אַל תָּבֹא נַפְשִׁי״ — meaning, my soul did not consent to being planted among them to be together with them when they gathered. And the intent of this is that that evil did not come from his power, as we have written above." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that he not be punished on account of the matter — either because his sons acted thus, or because he caused their jealousy by singling out his son among the sons. <b>בִּקְהָלָם</b> — when they gathered against him to kill him, as it is written (Genesis 37:20): ״לְכוּ וְנַהַרְגֵהוּ״ etc. — ״אַל תֵּחַד כְּבוֹדִי״: they were not called by my name, the children of Jacob, at all in that hour." "<b>Further,</b> it intends <b>אַל תֵּחַד</b> in the sense of joy (hedvah). Here he alluded to the griefs that overtook him and the departure of the Shekhinah from upon him — which is the aspect of כָּבוֹד (honor) — as they said of blessed memory (Tanhuma, Vayeshev) that from the time Joseph was lost to him, the Shekhinah did not rest upon him because of the sadness and grief. And his saying <b>כִּי בְאַפָּם</b> etc. means: the reason I say ״אַל תָּבֹא וְאַל תֵּחַד״ etc. is that when they gathered together to take a life they plotted, and this is his saying <b>הָרְגוּ אִישׁ</b>; and although we find that they did not kill him, they nevertheless agreed to kill him and cast him into a pit full of snakes, etc., to die there by several kinds of deaths." "<b>וּבִרְצוֹנָם</b> etc. Meaning: even at the time their anger rested, after they threw him into the pit and sat to eat bread, and Judah spoke to them words of appeasement and calm — even so they did evil, for they uprooted the ox, which is Joseph. For they said of blessed memory (Sotah 36b) that by means of the descent to Egypt and the evil incident of Potiphar's wife, ten tribes were uprooted from Joseph, for he was worthy of begetting twelve, and only two remained."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:31.007214363Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}